# Theory draft: drive and decay near the collapsed solution (working note, paper-repo)

Status: working material for the plan (story-repo #3) and for checking by experiment-repo (#3).
Not a delivery of results; numbers below come from a toy pilot in `notes/pilot/` only.

## Model
Linear JEPA, loss L = 1/2 E || P W x_c - sg(Wbar x_t) ||^2, weight decay lambda on W and P,
target Wbar = EMA of W (tau), Sigma_cc = E[x_c x_c^T], Sigma_tc = E[x_t x_c^T].
Assume Sigma_cc and Sigma_tc are co-diagonalizable (true for exchangeable views / masking of a
stationary signal): eigenpairs c_j (context variance) and s_j (context-target covariance) on a
common basis u_j. Regression coefficient (predictability) rho_j = s_j / c_j.

GD flow (stop-gradient, Wbar = W, i.e. tau -> 0):
  dW/dt = P^T (W Sigma_tc - P W Sigma_cc) - lambda W
  dP/dt = (W Sigma_tc - P W Sigma_cc) W^T - lambda P

## Mode reduction
If P is aligned with the modes (P u-modes have eigenvalue p_j; exact for P0 = p0 I with
orthogonal mode columns, approximately for small random W0) the system decouples into
2-D systems, one per direction j:

  dw_j/dt = w_j [ p_j s_j  -  p_j^2 c_j  -  lambda ]        (drive  D_j = p_j s_j ;
                                                              decay  K_j = p_j^2 c_j + lambda)
  dp_j/dt = w_j^2 ( s_j - p_j c_j ) - lambda p_j

Near collapse (w_j small) the predictor receives no signal (dp/dt is O(w^2)): p_j only decays,
p_j(t) ~ p0 exp(-lambda t), while each direction grows or shrinks at rate r_j(p) = p s_j - p^2 c_j - lambda.

Facts (to be proved in the appendix):
1. Growth window: r_j(p) > 0 iff p in (p_-, p_+), roots of c_j p^2 - s_j p + lambda = 0; non-empty iff
   s_j^2 / c_j > 4 lambda, i.e. rho_j s_j > 4 lambda. Same condition for existence of a non-collapsed
   fixed point (w*^2 = p*^2, p* = p_+). This is an upper bound on rank.
2. Collapse is decided early: (0,0) is stable for lambda > 0, so whether direction j survives is
   whether its initial condition (w_j(0), p0) lies in the basin of the non-trivial fixed point;
   the race is between the growth of w_j (rate r_j) and the drift of p out of the window
   (rate lambda, and later towards rho_j where r_j = -lambda).
3. Predicted rank = #{ j : (w0_j, p0) in basin_j } <= #{ j : s_j^2/c_j > 4 lambda }.
4. No stop-gradient: the target branch adds w (c_t - p s) to the decay; by Cauchy-Schwarz
   s^2 <= c c_t the rate is <= -lambda for all p: every direction collapses.
5. EMA target (tau): drive uses wbar; during growth at rate g, wbar ~ w/(1+g/kappa),
   kappa = (1-tau)/eta. The sign of r at g = 0 is unchanged, so EMA does not change the
   fixed-point condition but slows growth of fast directions relative to the predictor's drift,
   which moves basin boundaries (to be derived/tested).
6. Predictor design: (a) scalar/shared predictor couples directions through one p -> competition,
   directions with rho_j < p die; (b) bottleneck predictor of width k gives p_j = 0 outside its
   range -> r_j = -lambda, rank <= k; (c) predictor init scale p0: too small -> drive too weak
   vs lambda, too large -> decay p^2 c dominates (non-monotone rank in p0);
   (d) predictor learning rate: a fast predictor reaches rho_j where r_j = -lambda before w_j grows.
7. Masking ratio m: enters through (s_j(m), c_j(m)): higher masking lowers predictability of
   short-range directions (rho_j small) -> they fall below threshold first. Needs a data model.

## Pilot check (toy, not a result of the study)
`notes/pilot/mode_ode_vs_full.py`: D = d = 20, latent spectrum logspace(0,-2), context noise 0.05,
full-matrix GD on (W, P) vs the per-direction 2-D ODE above, 60k steps, eta = 0.01, w0 = 1e-3.
Number of directions with ||w_j|| > 1e-2 agrees exactly between the full system and the 2-D ODE in
all 12 settings of (p0, lambda) (`notes/pilot/mode_ode_vs_full.out`); the fixed-point bound (fact 1)
over-predicts, and the naive "r_j(p0) > 0" rule fails for p0 >= 1 (the predictor's decay moves p into
the window). So the theory must be the basin of the 2-D system, not the linear rate at init alone.

